From 4c424d5ca644d28e56426dcd92dd729a8c902aa4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 19:50:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08500/11362] Add opt-in CARNOT_ARC_GENERATOR_REQUIRE_CUDA
 hard-stop for CUDA-substrate ARC experiments

Operator directive: "We should be using the CUDA llama-server, not HIP/ROCm." A
transient 161 MiB VRAM shortfall on gpu1 exhausted _cuda_gpu_has_headroom's retry
window during exp6199's first run attempt, and _generator_server_and_env() silently
fell through to the ~2 tok/s iGPU HIP build -- no crash, no corrupted artifact (the
script only writes at the very end), but every induce call would have timed out and
looked like ordinary induction failure rather than an infrastructure fallback.

The HIP fallback itself is deliberate, existing, correct behavior for the conductor's
own routine ARC work (a slow generator beats no generator there) -- so this does not
remove it. Instead: GeneratorCudaRequiredError + an opt-in
CARNOT_ARC_GENERATOR_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 env var makes _generator_server_and_env() raise
instead of silently substituting HIP, scoped to callers that explicitly opt in. Default
unset, so the conductor's own generator resolution is byte-identical to before
(regression-tested). experiment_6199_gemma_think_mode_ab.py sets the flag and catches
the exception to emit honest_verdict: blocked_cuda_unavailable per the Pre-Launch
Preconditions Discipline, rather than running degraded and reporting a corrupted number.

5 new tests in test_generator_server_and_env_cuda_retry.py cover: default-unset
no-op, raise-on-busy-card, raise-on-missing-CUDA-binary, no-op-when-no-CUDA-pin-
requested, and no-op-on-success.

7 results/*.json artifacts get a freshness_acknowledgements entry: the only new
behavior is gated behind CARNOT_ARC_GENERATOR_REQUIRE_CUDA, which nothing in any of
these artifacts' generating scripts sets (grep-verified across the tree), so the new
branch is unreachable dead code from their perspective and their prior numbers stand.

Also deletes a stale test-suite-mutation-gate marker
(ops/.test_suite_mutation_runs/2077ff5ac4a847b9b02c2a71bacb8598.pending.json):
it attributed my deliberate edit to test_generator_server_and_env_cuda_retry.py to a
concurrent conductor pytest run (test_pipeline_extract.py/test_docs.py/test_agentic.py
-- unrelated modules, run_id ppid-279338 matches the conductor's own heartbeat pid).
Verified the diff is my own authored TestGeneratorRequireCuda class (10/10 tests pass)
before deleting, per the documented resolution path.

